Count the clauses now.

8
00:00:35,596 --> 00:00:38,526
You'll meet each one again,
as a line in a diff.

9
00:00:38,866 --> 00:00:43,152
The diff is 34 added lines in two files.
Your teammate is waiting for an approval.

10
00:00:43,192 --> 00:00:47,425
What do you write, in what order, and how
do you make sure you haven't missed the

11
00:00:47,465 --> 00:00:51,645
one line that matters? You need a checklist,
and you need to know what the tools

12
00:00:51,685 --> 00:00:53,122
will and won't find for you.

13
00:00:54,962 --> 00:00:58,869
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:00:58,909 --> 00:01:04,239
This video turns Module 29 into practice.
The last five videos gave you five attack

15
00:01:04,279 --> 00:01:09,286
classes and the controls for each. Here
they come together, in a file and in a

16
00:01:09,326 --> 00:01:09,934
procedure.

17
00:01:10,274 --> 00:01:15,286
Three words first. A workflow is a file
that tells GitHub Actions which jobs to run

18
00:01:15,326 --> 00:01:20,095
when an event happens. A pull request
proposes merging a branch, and a reviewer

19
00:01:20,135 --> 00:01:25,087
reads its diff, the lines it adds and
removes. And static analysis means reading a

20
00:01:25,127 --> 00:01:27,705
file for risky patterns
without running it.

21
00:01:28,045 --> 00:01:33,055
Five topics. Static analysis: what scanners
find in workflow files, and what they

22
00:01:33,095 --> 00:01:37,358
can't know. The platform changes of
2025 and 2026, as one dated list.

23
00:01:37,398 --> 00:01:42,533
Workflow 12, the course's secure-by-default
workflow, read with five questions that

24
00:01:42,573 --> 00:01:47,646
grep can answer for any workflow. AI agents
inside workflows, as the section gives

25
00:01:47,686 --> 00:01:52,759
the risks. And the review checklist of
section 21A.19: eleven items for every pull

26
00:01:52,799 --> 00:01:54,755
request that touches a workflow.

27
00:01:55,095 --> 00:01:59,937
The demonstration audits workflow 12 and
then opens the pull request from the hook.

28
00:01:59,977 --> 00:02:04,820
That pull request is constructed for the
exercise: the action and the host it names

29
00:02:04,860 --> 00:02:05,526
don't exist.

30
00:02:07,366 --> 00:02:08,944
Here's what you'll walk away with.

31
00:02:09,284 --> 00:02:10,697
After this video you can:

32
00:02:11,037 --> 00:02:14,529
say what static analysis of
workflows finds and what it cannot;

33
00:02:14,869 --> 00:02:20,336
state the platform changes of 2025
and 2026 that the section lists;

34
00:02:20,676 --> 00:02:23,257
justify every control of workflow 12;

35
00:02:23,597 --> 00:02:28,304
state the risks of an AI agent inside
a workflow as the section gives them;

36
00:02:28,644 --> 00:02:31,953
review a pull request that changes
workflows with the checklist.

37
00:02:33,793 --> 00:02:38,982
Static analysis. Four tools, from
the table of section 21A.14.

38
00:02:39,322 --> 00:02:44,475
CodeQL for workflows is part of GitHub
code scanning, the analysis of code for

39
00:02:44,515 --> 00:02:49,535
vulnerabilities and coding errors. It
has been generally available since the

40
00:02:49,575 --> 00:02:55,061
twenty-second of April 2025, and default
setup enables it when workflow files exist

41
00:02:55,101 --> 00:03:00,120
on the default branch. It finds, in the
changelog's words, "missing required

42
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:05,247
permissions, dangerous inputs without
proper validation, and script injection

43
00:03:05,287 --> 00:03:10,373
vulnerabilities", and since CodeQL 2.26.4
also mutable references to reusable

44
00:03:10,413 --> 00:03:11,039
workflows.

45
00:03:11,379 --> 00:03:16,822
zizmor is a third-party tool. Version
1.30.1 was observed on the first of October

46
00:03:16,862 --> 00:03:22,372
2026. Its audits have names you'll recognize
from this module: template injection,

47
00:03:22,412 --> 00:03:27,381
dangerous triggers, excessive permissions,
unpinned uses, cache poisoning,

48
00:03:27,421 --> 00:03:32,931
overprovisioned secrets, secrets inherit,
self-hosted runner, Dependabot cooldown,

49
00:03:32,971 --> 00:03:33,675
and others.

50
00:03:34,015 --> 00:03:37,467
actionlint is a third-party
tool, at version 1.7.12.

51
00:03:37,507 --> 00:03:42,972
Syntax and expression type checks, shellcheck
integration, and script injection by

52
00:03:43,012 --> 00:03:45,926
untrusted inputs and
hard-coded credentials.

53
00:03:46,266 --> 00:03:51,025
OpenSSF Scorecard is a third-party tool
whose results can surface in code scanning,

54
00:03:51,065 --> 00:03:55,535
with the checks Dangerous-Workflow,
Token-Permissions and Pinned-Dependencies.

55
00:03:55,875 --> 00:04:00,738
Neither zizmor nor actionlint is installed
in the course's environment, so no output

56
00:04:00,778 --> 00:04:01,263
is shown.

57
00:04:01,603 --> 00:04:05,589
Quick quiz. A scanner runs on your
workflow and reports nothing.

58
00:04:05,629 --> 00:04:10,874
What have you learned? A, the workflow is
secure. B, the patterns that scanner knows

59
00:04:10,914 --> 00:04:12,509
didn't match. Your answer?

60
00:04:17,349 --> 00:04:21,819
B. The textbook gives the limit in one
sentence: a scanner finds patterns.

61
00:04:21,859 --> 00:04:26,025
It doesn't know which of your jobs
holds the credential that matters.

62
00:04:26,065 --> 00:04:30,900
Its advice: run one in CI, your automated
checks, as a required check, and still

63
00:04:30,940 --> 00:04:31,753
read the file.

64
00:04:32,093 --> 00:04:37,635
The platform changes of 2025 and 2026. One
caution first, in the textbook's words.

65
00:04:37,675 --> 00:04:43,013
The dates are sourced. Pairing each change
with an incident is the researchers'

66
00:04:43,053 --> 00:04:48,050
inference, not GitHub's statement.
GitHub's changelog posts don't name the

67
00:04:48,090 --> 00:04:48,730
incidents.

68
00:04:49,070 --> 00:04:54,244
The twenty-second of April 2025: CodeQL
analysis of workflow files generally

69
00:04:54,284 --> 00:04:59,527
available. The fifteenth of August 2025:
the allowed-actions policy can block

70
00:04:59,567 --> 00:05:05,152
entries and require full commit pins. The
twenty-eighth of October 2025: immutable

71
00:05:05,192 --> 00:05:09,543
releases, whose tag can no longer
be moved, generally available.

72
00:05:09,583 --> 00:05:15,168
The eighth of December 2025: pull_request_target
always uses the workflow from the

73
00:05:15,208 --> 00:05:19,970
default branch, and environment branch
rules follow the execution ref.

74
00:05:20,310 --> 00:05:24,536
The fifth of February 2026: action
allow-listing on all plans.

75
00:05:24,576 --> 00:05:30,040
The twenty-third of April and the fifteenth
of July 2026: immutable OIDC subject

76
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:33,686
claims, opt-in and then
default for new repositories.

77
00:05:33,726 --> 00:05:38,984
The eighteenth of June and the twentieth
of July 2026: the checkout action at

78
00:05:39,024 --> 00:05:43,937
version 7 refuses fork pull request
code under privileged triggers, then

79
00:05:43,977 --> 00:05:49,441
back-ported. The twenty-sixth of June
2026: a read-only default-branch cache for

80
00:05:49,481 --> 00:05:54,808
low-trust triggers. The fourteenth of
July 2026: a default cooldown, a waiting

81
00:05:54,848 --> 00:05:57,491
period, for Dependabot version updates.

82
00:05:57,831 --> 00:06:03,270
The twenty-eighth of July 2026: runs
"identified as potentially malicious" are held

83
00:06:03,310 --> 00:06:08,683
until a collaborator with write access
approves them, in public repositories only,

84
00:06:08,723 --> 00:06:12,842
with no configuration. The tenth
of September 2026: cache-mode.

85
00:06:12,882 --> 00:06:17,794
The seventeenth of September 2026:
workflow execution protections generally

86
00:06:17,834 --> 00:06:23,273
available. And the second of November 2026:
the date GitHub announced for enforcing

87
00:06:23,313 --> 00:06:28,752
the default rule that disables pull_request_target
in affected public repositories.

88
00:06:28,792 --> 00:06:31,789
Check the changelog on
the day you watch this.

89
00:06:32,129 --> 00:06:37,029
Unverified: the heuristics behind the holds
on suspicious runs aren't documented.

90
00:06:37,069 --> 00:06:39,042
Don't count on them as a control.

91
00:06:39,382 --> 00:06:42,487
You've met every row but one
in the last five videos.

92
00:06:42,527 --> 00:06:47,472
Seen as one list, it says something about
direction: the platform is moving defaults

93
00:06:47,512 --> 00:06:52,457
toward what this module teaches. And about
a limit: each row has a "does not cover",

94
00:06:52,497 --> 00:06:54,890
and none of them reads your file for you.

95
00:06:55,230 --> 00:07:00,562
Workflow 12. The file puts the controls in
one place: a test job, a job that reads

96
00:07:00,602 --> 00:07:06,000
the pull request title, a dependency review,
and a staging deployment through OIDC,

97
00:07:06,040 --> 00:07:11,176
the exchange that replaces a stored cloud
key. It was assembled from documented

98
00:07:11,216 --> 00:07:16,286
syntax and input names and parse-checked.
It was not executed on GitHub by the

99
00:07:16,326 --> 00:07:18,382
author. Lab 29.2 has you run it.

100
00:07:18,722 --> 00:07:23,737
The textbook reads it with five questions,
which grep can answer for any workflow.

101
00:07:23,777 --> 00:07:28,360
Which events start it? What may the
token do? Whose code runs, and is every

102
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:33,230
reference a full commit ID? Where does
an expression appear, and is any of them

103
00:07:33,270 --> 00:07:36,373
inside a script? Which
stored secrets does it read?

104
00:07:36,413 --> 00:07:38,346
You'll run all five in a moment.

105
00:07:38,686 --> 00:07:41,302
Then the controls, each
with the threat it answers.

106
00:07:41,642 --> 00:07:46,763
permissions: contents: read at the top, an
empty map for the title job, and per-job

107
00:07:46,803 --> 00:07:50,743
additions below: against a
compromised step using a write token.

108
00:07:51,083 --> 00:07:54,992
on: pull_request: against fork
code running next to secrets.

109
00:07:55,332 --> 00:07:59,587
Every uses is a commit ID with a
version comment: against a moved tag.

110
00:07:59,927 --> 00:08:04,618
persist-credentials: false on every checkout:
against later steps using the token

111
00:08:04,658 --> 00:08:05,318
through Git.

112
00:08:05,658 --> 00:08:08,911
The title only under env:
against script injection.

113
00:08:09,251 --> 00:08:13,637
The deployment if requires a push to main:
against unreviewed code reaching the

114
00:08:13,677 --> 00:08:14,478
cloud identity.

115
00:08:14,818 --> 00:08:19,724
environment: staging: against a deployment
without the environment's rules.

116
00:08:19,764 --> 00:08:21,768
It also fixes the OIDC subject.

117
00:08:22,108 --> 00:08:27,204
id-token: write on one job only: against
any other job requesting a cloud token.

118
00:08:27,544 --> 00:08:32,316
cache-mode: none on the deployment job:
against a poisoned cache executing next to

119
00:08:32,356 --> 00:08:33,431
the cloud identity.

120
00:08:33,771 --> 00:08:37,730
timeout-minutes and concurrency:
against runaway or overlapping runs.

121
00:08:38,070 --> 00:08:43,049
And what the file doesn't do, which the
textbook lists as carefully as what it does.

122
00:08:43,089 --> 00:08:46,155
It doesn't attest the build.
It doesn't scan itself.

123
00:08:46,195 --> 00:08:51,114
Add CodeQL default setup for that. And the
staging role's trust policy lives in the

124
00:08:51,154 --> 00:08:54,161
cloud account, where the
workflow can't enforce it.

125
00:08:54,501 --> 00:08:59,329
AI agents inside workflows. An agent in
a workflow, whether an LLM reviewer, a

126
00:08:59,369 --> 00:09:04,384
triage bot or a coding agent, combines
parts three and four of the model in a new

127
00:09:04,424 --> 00:09:09,502
form. An LLM is a large language model, a
program that reads text and writes text.

128
00:09:09,542 --> 00:09:13,434
The agent reads attacker-controlled
text and holds credentials.

129
00:09:13,474 --> 00:09:18,177
And for an agent, text is instruction.
The textbook's sentence: no env trick

130
00:09:18,217 --> 00:09:20,486
separates data from code in a prompt.

131
00:09:20,826 --> 00:09:26,115
Two findings from the report, with their
flags. An automated account ran a week-long

132
00:09:26,155 --> 00:09:30,936
campaign in February and March 2026 that
combined pull_request_target abuse,

133
00:09:30,976 --> 00:09:36,265
branch-name and filename injection, and
prompt injection, instructions hidden in the

134
00:09:36,305 --> 00:09:39,056
text an agent reads,
against an AI reviewer.

135
00:09:39,396 --> 00:09:44,381
And researchers reported in April 2026 that
AI coding agents run as GitHub Actions

136
00:09:44,421 --> 00:09:49,161
could be steered by text in pull request
titles, issue bodies or comments into

137
00:09:49,201 --> 00:09:54,063
revealing CI secrets. The textbook marks
the second: this is secondary reporting

138
00:09:54,103 --> 00:09:56,943
only, and the primary
write-up was not fetched.

139
00:09:57,283 --> 00:10:00,854
The report's recommendation,
which it labels as an inference.

140
00:10:00,894 --> 00:10:05,116
An agent in a workflow gets a dedicated
low-privilege, spend-capped key.

141
00:10:05,156 --> 00:10:09,675
No write token unless required. And it
doesn't run automatically on untrusted

142
00:10:09,715 --> 00:10:14,411
contributions. Then the chapter's own
formulation, which is the one to remember:

143
00:10:14,451 --> 00:10:19,324
give the agent's job permissions as if the
pull request author had written the job,

144
00:10:19,364 --> 00:10:21,870
because through the
prompt they partly did.

145
00:10:22,210 --> 00:10:28,748
The review checklist. Use it on every pull
request that touches .github/workflows/,

146
00:10:28,788 --> 00:10:33,106
an action definition, or a
script that a workflow runs.

147
00:10:33,146 --> 00:10:34,137
Eleven items.

148
00:10:34,477 --> 00:10:38,913
One, trigger. Does the change add or
keep pull_request_target, workflow_run,

149
00:10:38,953 --> 00:10:43,802
issue_comment, issues or discussion? If so,
which outsider-controlled input reaches

150
00:10:43,842 --> 00:10:44,273
the job?

151
00:10:44,613 --> 00:10:48,839
Two, checkout. Any ref or
repository taken from the event?

152
00:10:48,879 --> 00:10:54,870
Any allow-unsafe-pr-checkout? Any git fetch
or gh pr checkout of pull request code

153
00:10:54,910 --> 00:10:56,341
in a privileged job?

154
00:10:56,681 --> 00:11:01,086
Three, permissions. Is there a
top-level permissions block, read-only?

155
00:11:01,126 --> 00:11:06,165
Is each write scope on the one job that
needs it? Did the change widen anything?

156
00:11:06,505 --> 00:11:12,134
Four, expressions. Is there any expression
inside run, or inside an input that is

157
00:11:12,174 --> 00:11:15,494
evaluated as code? Including
multi-line scripts?

158
00:11:15,834 --> 00:11:21,210
Five, actions. Is every uses a 40-character
commit ID from the action's own

159
00:11:21,250 --> 00:11:27,276
repository, with a version comment? Is a
new action necessary, and who maintains it?

160
00:11:27,616 --> 00:11:33,217
Six, remote code. Any download piped into
a shell, or an installer fetched without a

161
00:11:33,257 --> 00:11:33,821
checksum?

162
00:11:34,161 --> 00:11:39,853
Seven, secrets. Is each secret referenced
in the narrowest place: one step, in a job

163
00:11:39,893 --> 00:11:45,381
behind an environment? Could OIDC replace
it? Does any secret appear on a command

164
00:11:45,421 --> 00:11:45,722
line?

165
00:11:46,062 --> 00:11:51,290
Eight, privileged jobs. For each job that
publishes, deploys or holds id-token:

166
00:11:51,330 --> 00:11:56,826
write: does it run only on trusted refs,
behind an environment, with cache-mode set

167
00:11:56,866 --> 00:12:01,428
to none or read, and with no artifact
from an untrusted run executed?

168
00:12:01,768 --> 00:12:05,714
Nine, runner. Did runs-on
change to a self-hosted label?

169
00:12:05,754 --> 00:12:06,925
For which events?

170
00:12:07,265 --> 00:12:12,590
Ten, credentials in Git. persist-credentials:
false wherever the job doesn't push?

171
00:12:12,930 --> 00:12:18,253
Eleven, process. Was the change reviewed by
a code owner of the workflows directory,

172
00:12:18,293 --> 00:12:23,426
and does a ruleset require that? Does a
workflow scanner run on the pull request?

173
00:12:25,266 --> 00:12:30,304
A picture helps. Hold workflow 12 in mind
as a reference shape, and review other

174
00:12:30,344 --> 00:12:32,399
workflows as differences from it.

175
00:12:32,739 --> 00:12:38,053
The shape has two zones. An open zone,
where outsiders' code and text are allowed

176
00:12:38,093 --> 00:12:41,688
in: the test job, the title
job, the dependency review.

177
00:12:41,728 --> 00:12:47,108
In that zone the token is read-only or
absent, there are no secrets, and untrusted

178
00:12:47,148 --> 00:12:52,396
text is only ever data. And a closed zone,
the deployment job, where an identity

179
00:12:52,436 --> 00:12:57,684
lives. Nothing from outside gets in: only
a push to main, behind an environment,

180
00:12:57,724 --> 00:12:58,610
with no cache.

181
00:12:58,950 --> 00:13:01,913
Every item of the checklist
asks one of two things.

182
00:13:01,953 --> 00:13:05,681
Did this change let something from
outside into the closed zone?

183
00:13:05,721 --> 00:13:08,802
Or did it move something
valuable into the open zone?

184
00:13:09,142 --> 00:13:14,452
Where the model needs a warning, and the
textbook gives it in section 21A.21: the

185
00:13:14,492 --> 00:13:19,933
edge case behind most incidents is two
safe-looking workflows that share something.

186
00:13:19,973 --> 00:13:25,348
A low-privilege workflow that can write a
cache, an artifact, a label or a branch,

187
00:13:25,388 --> 00:13:30,301
and a high-privilege workflow that
trusts it. So the zones aren't per file.

188
00:13:30,341 --> 00:13:32,018
Review workflows as a set.

189
00:13:33,858 --> 00:13:38,743
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper. Write
the names of workflow 12's four jobs:

190
00:13:38,783 --> 00:13:42,817
test, the title job, dependency
review, and the staging deployment.

191
00:13:42,857 --> 00:13:47,803
Circle the one that holds an identity. How
many controls would you put in front of

192
00:13:47,843 --> 00:13:49,384
it? Say a number out loud.

193
00:13:54,224 --> 00:13:58,948
Check your page against the outline.
Above the deploy job is the open zone.

194
00:13:58,988 --> 00:14:04,157
The deploy job is the closed zone. Count
the brackets on it: five, and each closes

195
00:14:04,197 --> 00:14:04,919
one road in.

196
00:14:06,759 --> 00:14:10,915
Questions 1 and 2: which events
start it, and what may the token do?

197
00:14:14,255 --> 00:14:19,085
pull_request, not the privileged
trigger: fork code runs without secrets.

198
00:14:19,125 --> 00:14:23,954
Then four permissions blocks. At line
21 the workflow token is read-only.

199
00:14:23,994 --> 00:14:27,823
At line 60 the title job has
an empty map: nothing at all.

200
00:14:27,863 --> 00:14:33,092
At line 104 the deployment job adds
id-token: write, and it's the only one that

201
00:14:33,132 --> 00:14:33,426
does.

202
00:14:33,766 --> 00:14:35,676
Question 3: whose code runs?

203
00:14:36,016 --> 00:14:39,668
Six references, six commit
IDs, all from the pin file.

204
00:14:39,708 --> 00:14:45,343
Exit status 1 from the second command means
that grep -v found nothing to print: no

205
00:14:45,383 --> 00:14:48,761
reference that isn't forty
hexadecimal characters.

206
00:14:49,101 --> 00:14:51,657
Question 4: where does
an expression appear?

207
00:14:51,997 --> 00:14:56,127
Eight lines will appear. Classify
each by where the value lands.

208
00:14:56,167 --> 00:15:01,340
Three bins: evaluated by Actions and never
reaching a shell, an action input, or

209
00:15:01,380 --> 00:15:05,250
text for a script. And which
one carries an outsider's text?

210
00:15:05,290 --> 00:15:06,292
Say it out loud.

211
00:15:11,132 --> 00:15:16,470
Lines 26, 27, 56, 77 and 96 are a concurrency
group and if conditions: evaluated by

212
00:15:16,510 --> 00:15:21,589
Actions, never reaching a shell. Lines
122 and 123 are action inputs taken from

213
00:15:21,629 --> 00:15:25,930
repository variables, which only
people with repository access set.

214
00:15:25,970 --> 00:15:31,243
Line 66 is the one untrusted value, the
pull request title, and it sits under env.

215
00:15:31,283 --> 00:15:33,706
No expression appears inside a script.

216
00:15:34,046 --> 00:15:37,026
Question 5: which stored
secrets does it read?

217
00:15:37,366 --> 00:15:41,597
None: exit status 1. The
workflow reads no stored secret.

218
00:15:41,637 --> 00:15:47,666
Its only credential beyond the job token
is the OIDC token of the deployment job.

219
00:15:48,006 --> 00:15:53,486
Five questions, about a minute. Now use the
same eyes on a file that isn't secure by

220
00:15:53,526 --> 00:15:54,011
default.

221
00:15:54,351 --> 00:16:01,203
The review. Replay labs/run ch21a/lab-29-3-review.
A repository with a sound ci.yml

222
00:16:01,243 --> 00:16:07,680
on main, and a branch by a teammate with
the description you read in the hook.

223
00:16:08,020 --> 00:16:14,094
One commit, 4b55a46, by Ravi. Two
files: ci.yml changed, release.yml new.

224
00:16:14,134 --> 00:16:18,114
Here are the clauses from
the opening, as lines.

225
00:16:18,454 --> 00:16:22,131
Hold the checklist beside the
diff. Don't look for "the bug".

226
00:16:22,171 --> 00:16:27,005
Go through the items in order, one to
eleven, and for each ask whether this diff

227
00:16:27,045 --> 00:16:30,173
touches it. How many items
does this one file touch?

228
00:16:30,213 --> 00:16:31,452
Make your prediction.

229
00:16:36,292 --> 00:16:41,116
I won't give you the findings. They're
the lab. But I'll show you how to read.

230
00:16:41,156 --> 00:16:46,291
Take the first hunk line by line and name
the checklist item for each changed line,

231
00:16:46,331 --> 00:16:51,466
without judging yet. The trigger line: item
one. The permissions lines: item three.

232
00:16:51,806 --> 00:16:56,605
The new env block at workflow level: item
seven. runs-on: item nine, and notice

233
00:16:56,645 --> 00:17:01,689
which line disappeared next to it. The lines
under the checkout: items two and ten.

234
00:17:01,729 --> 00:17:06,405
Then the added steps at the bottom: there
are three, and they belong to three

235
00:17:06,445 --> 00:17:07,385
different items.

236
00:17:07,725 --> 00:17:12,465
Notice also what a diff hides. The lines
that didn't change are still in the file,

237
00:17:12,505 --> 00:17:16,487
and their meaning has changed because
the trigger above them changed.

238
00:17:16,527 --> 00:17:19,926
uv sync --locked and uv run
pytest were harmless yesterday.

239
00:17:19,966 --> 00:17:24,647
Ask item two's question about them today.
That's why the lab has you read the new

240
00:17:24,687 --> 00:17:26,163
files whole with git show.

241
00:17:29,503 --> 00:17:34,192
A new file of seventeen lines. For a
new file, start with what is absent.

242
00:17:34,232 --> 00:17:39,569
Run the five questions in your head: trigger,
permissions, references, expressions,

243
00:17:39,609 --> 00:17:43,261
secrets. One of the five has
no line in this file at all.

244
00:17:43,301 --> 00:17:46,759
Then item eight, because this
is a job that publishes.

245
00:17:47,099 --> 00:17:53,784
git grep with a branch name searches the
files as they're on that branch, without

246
00:17:53,824 --> 00:17:55,113
checking it out.

247
00:17:55,453 --> 00:18:00,116
Twelve lines from one pattern search. This
is the scanner's view: places to look.

248
00:18:00,156 --> 00:18:04,993
It found the patterns it was given. It didn't
find the missing lines, and it doesn't

249
00:18:05,033 --> 00:18:07,838
know which job holds the
credential that matters.

250
00:18:08,178 --> 00:18:10,735
One more thing a reviewer
must get right: the base.

251
00:18:11,075 --> 00:18:16,547
With two dots, the stat shows a third file,
README.md, with two deletions the author

252
00:18:16,587 --> 00:18:19,959
never made: main moved after
the branch was created.

253
00:18:20,299 --> 00:18:24,960
With three dots you see the branch against
its merge base, the most recent commit

254
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,978
both histories share, as
the pull request page does.

255
00:18:28,018 --> 00:18:32,562
Review the three-dot diff, or you'll
comment on changes that aren't in the pull

256
00:18:32,602 --> 00:18:33,027
request.

257
00:18:33,367 --> 00:18:38,193
Lab 29.3 is done in the lab shell;
its product is a file, review.md.

258
00:18:38,233 --> 00:18:44,061
If you then want to practise the form on
GitHub, open any pull request on your own

259
00:18:44,101 --> 00:18:49,714
practice repository that changes a workflow,
for example the one from Lab 29.2.

260
00:18:49,754 --> 00:18:53,721
The interface changes, so name
what you see by function.

261
00:18:54,061 --> 00:18:59,150
Find where a comment is attached to a single
line of the diff, and where the overall

262
00:18:59,190 --> 00:19:02,569
verdict is chosen: approve,
comment, or request changes.

263
00:19:02,609 --> 00:19:07,698
gh pr checkout gives you the branch locally.
The textbook labels it 🟢 SAFE, since it

264
00:19:07,738 --> 00:19:12,583
changes local files and refs only. And
remember from the checklist that the same

265
00:19:12,623 --> 00:19:16,612
command is a finding when it appears
inside a privileged workflow.

266
00:19:18,452 --> 00:19:20,100
Five mistakes to watch for.

267
00:19:20,440 --> 00:19:25,354
Treating a green scanner as a review. Root
cause: a scanner finds patterns; it does

268
00:19:25,394 --> 00:19:30,130
not know which job holds the credential
that matters, and it does not find lines

269
00:19:30,170 --> 00:19:31,144
that are missing.

270
00:19:31,484 --> 00:19:36,369
Reviewing only the changed lines. Root cause:
an unchanged step means something else

271
00:19:36,409 --> 00:19:39,887
after the trigger above it
changed; read the new file whole.

272
00:19:40,227 --> 00:19:44,691
Reviewing one workflow at a time. Root
cause: most incidents involve two

273
00:19:44,731 --> 00:19:49,445
safe-looking workflows that share a
cache, an artifact, a label or a branch.

274
00:19:49,785 --> 00:19:52,948
Giving an agent's job the
permissions the team would have.

275
00:19:52,988 --> 00:19:57,256
Root cause: the agent reads attacker-controlled
text, and for an agent text is

276
00:19:57,296 --> 00:20:01,509
instruction; through the prompt the pull
request author partly wrote the job.

277
00:20:01,849 --> 00:20:06,803
Reading a two-dot diff of the pull request.
Root cause: two dots compare the tips,

278
00:20:06,843 --> 00:20:11,250
so changes made on the base branch
appear as if the author had made them.

279
00:20:13,090 --> 00:20:18,002
Now, out of the lab. A team adds an LLM
reviewer to its repository of evaluation

280
00:20:18,042 --> 00:20:22,582
code. The workflow runs on pull requests,
gives the agent the diff and the

281
00:20:22,622 --> 00:20:27,782
description, and lets it post a review. To
post, it needs a write scope, and to call

282
00:20:27,822 --> 00:20:32,486
the model it needs an API key. The first
version runs on every pull request,

283
00:20:32,526 --> 00:20:37,624
including those from forks, on the privileged
trigger so that the key is available.

284
00:20:37,964 --> 00:20:43,320
Run the checklist. Item one: a privileged
trigger, and the outsider-controlled input

285
00:20:43,360 --> 00:20:47,881
is the entire content of the pull
request, handed to the agent as text.

286
00:20:47,921 --> 00:20:51,543
Item seven: a provider key in
a job that reads that text.

287
00:20:51,583 --> 00:20:54,241
Item three: a write scope in the same job.

288
00:20:54,581 --> 00:20:57,693
The textbook's recommendation
applies point by point.

289
00:20:57,733 --> 00:21:01,915
A dedicated low-privilege, spend-capped
key, so that a leak is bounded.

290
00:21:01,955 --> 00:21:06,851
No write token unless required: for example,
the agent writes its review to the job

291
00:21:06,891 --> 00:21:10,359
summary, and a separate job
without the model key posts it.

292
00:21:10,399 --> 00:21:15,295
And it doesn't run automatically on untrusted
contributions: a maintainer starts it

293
00:21:15,335 --> 00:21:18,328
for a fork's pull request
after reading the change.

294
00:21:18,668 --> 00:21:23,604
The team's rule afterwards is the chapter's
sentence, pinned in the repository: give

295
00:21:23,644 --> 00:21:28,224
the agent's job permissions as if the
pull request author had written the job.

296
00:21:30,064 --> 00:21:36,824
Your turn. Do Lab 29.3, "Review a pull
request that changes workflows", in

297
00:21:36,864 --> 00:21:41,418
lab-manual/m29-actions-security.md,
in labs/shell.

298
00:21:41,758 --> 00:21:46,523
Write review.md: one comment per finding,
each with the file and line, a severity,

299
00:21:46,563 --> 00:21:51,036
what the line does, what an attacker
could achieve, and the requested change.

300
00:21:51,076 --> 00:21:55,606
Before you start, predict how many findings
there are, and afterwards compare.

301
00:21:55,646 --> 00:22:00,529
Finish with a verdict and a two-sentence
summary for the author that is specific and

302
00:22:00,569 --> 00:22:03,225
not hostile: they were
solving a real problem.

303
00:22:03,565 --> 00:22:10,546
The challenge is Exercise 29.7, "Benchmark
scores on fork pull requests", in

304
00:22:10,586 --> 00:22:14,057
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

305
00:22:15,897 --> 00:22:19,113
Read the question on screen.
Say your answer out loud.

306
00:22:23,953 --> 00:22:28,135
This is the broadest question of the
module, and a weak answer is a list of

307
00:22:28,175 --> 00:22:32,863
settings. A strong answer starts from the
one question, whose code, whose text, what

308
00:22:32,903 --> 00:22:37,422
can it reach, and organizes the controls
by the five parts of the model, with one

309
00:22:37,462 --> 00:22:39,617
sentence each on the threat it answers.

310
00:22:39,957 --> 00:22:44,414
It separates what is in the file from what
is in settings, and names the process

311
00:22:44,454 --> 00:22:48,911
control that protects the files themselves.
It says what remains outside: what a

312
00:22:48,951 --> 00:22:52,059
scanner can't know, and what
lives in the cloud account.

313
00:22:52,399 --> 00:22:56,917
The follow-up asks which controls the
platform can enforce for you and which still

314
00:22:56,957 --> 00:23:01,530
depend on a person reading the file. You
have the policy table and the checklist to

315
00:23:01,570 --> 00:23:02,197
divide them.

316
00:23:04,037 --> 00:23:04,964
Let's land this.

317
00:23:05,304 --> 00:23:06,964
You should now be able to say:

318
00:23:07,304 --> 00:23:12,300
A scanner finds patterns in workflow files;
it does not know which job holds the

319
00:23:12,340 --> 00:23:16,329
credential that matters, so you
run one and still read the file.

320
00:23:16,669 --> 00:23:22,755
From 2025 to 2026 the platform moved its
defaults toward these controls, with 2

321
00:23:22,795 --> 00:23:28,881
November 2026 as the next announced date;
each change has something it does not

322
00:23:28,921 --> 00:23:29,347
cover.

323
00:23:29,687 --> 00:23:34,811
Workflow 12 keeps outsiders' code and text
in jobs without privilege, and keeps its

324
00:23:34,851 --> 00:23:39,664
one identity in a job that only trusted
code can reach, behind an environment,

325
00:23:39,704 --> 00:23:40,660
without a cache.

326
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:45,586
An agent in a workflow reads attacker-controlled
text and holds credentials; give

327
00:23:45,626 --> 00:23:49,299
its job permissions as if the pull
request author had written it.

328
00:23:49,639 --> 00:23:54,265
The checklist has eleven items: trigger,
checkout, permissions, expressions,

329
00:23:54,305 --> 00:23:59,422
actions, remote code, secrets, privileged
jobs, runner, credentials in Git, process.

330
00:24:01,262 --> 00:24:09,924
Read sections 21A.14 to 21A.17 and
21A.19 to 21A.22 of Chapter 21A.

331
00:24:09,964 --> 00:24:13,950
Do the Practice section 21A.24.

332
00:24:14,290 --> 00:24:19,036
Today you audited a workflow with five
questions and read a risky diff with eleven.

333
00:24:19,076 --> 00:24:21,976
Write that review in the
lab before the next video.

334
00:24:22,016 --> 00:24:26,762
Next time: case studies in Actions security,
with what happened, the root cause and

335
00:24:26,802 --> 00:24:30,452
the lesson. Until then, look at
the state first and type second.

336
00:24:30,492 --> 00:24:31,836
See you in the next one.
